Only silence the subagent "done" alert, and put the rest of the child's alerts back

The previous commit read the request as "a child is worth a buzz only when it is
stuck" and dropped a subagent's error and terminal bell along with its idle alert. That
was too much: the thing to stop is the "Done: <title>" notification, and everything
else about a child still matters.

So the blocking flag is gone and `alert` is back to one rule — post unless the reader is
looking at it. The idle alert is silenced where it is raised instead: a subagent
finishing its turn is not news, because the parent's turn is what the reader is waiting
for and the child's result arrives in the parent's transcript anyway. A dispatch of
three children ended in four notifications, three of them about work nobody was
supervising directly. The row still takes its green "done" dot, which is the quiet half
of the same signal.

A question or an approval still speaks for a child, because they stall it where the
parent's screen cannot show that the header counts a waiting child as running. An error
or a bell still speaks for it too, because those are events rather than a status.

The `visibleWith` half of that commit stands: opening a parent settles its subagents'
alerts and suppresses them while it is in front. 342 green.